  <!-- ============ MODULE 1 ============ -->
  <section class="chapter module" id="m1">
    <div class="chapter-label">Module 1</div>
    <h2>Mine Your Experience: The 25-Year Asset Inventory</h2>
    <p class="module-dek">The raw materials are already in you. First we get them on paper.</p>

    <p>Here's a line I overheard on a podcast that changed how I see work: <em>"We are all just storytellers and toolmakers."</em></p>
    <p>Strip any career down and that's what's left. We run into problems. We build or borrow tools to solve them. Then we tell the story of how we solved them, to help the next person. That's not just work. That's the whole engine of getting paid on your own terms.</p>
    <p>Which means your 25 years isn't a sunk cost you're walking away from. It's a deposit. A pile of problems you've already learned to solve that other people are still stuck on. The trouble is you've solved them so many times you've stopped noticing they're worth anything.</p>
    <div class="pull">The skills you take for granted are usually the ones somebody else will pay for.</div>
    <p>So before we aim anywhere, we lay it all out on the table. You already started. That's the box up top. The four-part brain dump is exactly what's in it: <strong>your roles, the tools you know, the projects that made or saved money, and the hobbies where you've quietly gotten good.</strong> If you skipped it, scroll up and rough it in now. It feeds everything below.</p>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="ex-tag">The Exercise</div>
      <p>With your raw materials filled in above, run them through the prompt below. It uses the <strong>problem → tool → story</strong> framework to surface marketable problems you solve that you've stopped seeing. Copy it, paste it into your AI, read what it hands back.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #1 · Marketable Skills</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">Hello AI, I am a Gen X professional keen on integrating my skills into effective marketing opportunities using the problem, tool, and story framework. Here's a snapshot of my background:

Professional Experience: <span class="ph" data-ph="roles">[your past jobs and roles]</span>

Key Skills and Strengths: <span class="ph" data-ph="skills">[your major skills and strengths]</span>

Problems Solved: <span class="ph" data-ph="projects">[significant problems you've solved]</span>

Tools or Systems Created: <span class="ph" data-ph="tools">[tools or systems you've built or improved]</span>

Passions and Interests: <span class="ph" data-ph="hobbies">[your passions and interests]</span>

Based on this information, can you assist me in:
1. Identifying Marketable Problems: Pinpointing problems that I've solved, which are relatable and have market potential.
2. Tool Development Opportunities: Assessing how my skills can be used to create or improve tools or systems that address these problems.
3. Crafting Compelling Stories: Formulating stories that effectively communicate how I've solved these problems using specific tools or systems, which can engage and inspire potential clients or employers.
4. Aligning Skills with Market Needs: Offering suggestions on how my unique skill set and interests can align with current marketing trends and demands.

I'm looking for practical strategies to use my background in a marketing-centric career or business venture, focusing on the problem, tool, and story approach. Thank you!</div>
    </div>

    <div class="outcome">
      <span class="oc-tag">You walk away with</span>
      <p>A written inventory of your marketable assets. Concrete proof, on the page, that you're not starting from zero.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MODULE 2 ============ -->
  <section class="chapter module" id="m2">
    <div class="chapter-label">Module 2</div>
    <h2>Aim Where AI Can't Follow: The Rory Test</h2>
    <p class="module-dek">Don't build a future on ground that's about to disappear.</p>

    <p>Before you fall in love with any direction, you pressure-test it against one question: <strong>can a machine do this cheaper than you next year?</strong></p>
    <p>There's a map for this. I sketched it in my notebook one weekend. It's called the "AI-Proof Quadrant" (credit to Perry Marshall). Two axes: <strong>Productive ↔ Unproductive</strong> and <strong>Predictable ↔ Unpredictable.</strong> Three of the four corners are the kill zone:</p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Unproductive &amp; Predictable:</strong> bureaucracy, middle management, admin. AI takes this easily.</li>
      <li><strong>Productive &amp; Predictable:</strong> coding, data entry, logistics. AI takes this.</li>
      <li><strong>Productive &amp; Unpredictable:</strong> strategy optimization. Agentic AI is eating this faster than anyone expected.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/compass/ai-proof-quadrant.jpg" alt="The AI-Proof Quadrant sketched on a yellow legal pad, showing the three AI kill zones and the human 'celebration' zone">
      <figcaption>My actual notebook sketch of the AI-Proof Quadrant. The top-right corner is the only safe harbor.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>That leaves one safe harbor: the top-right corner, <strong>Unproductive &amp; Unpredictable.</strong> The human zone. Corporate culture hates it because it looks like waste. But it's the only ground the robots can't take. Perry's six traits that live there: <em>analog, communal, gastronomic, artistic, altruistic,</em> and <em>subversive.</em></p>
    <div class="pull">AI without experience is just expensive autocomplete. Your judgment is the thing it can't fake.</div>
    <p>That's why your 25 years matters here. Experience and judgment live in the safe harbor. The trick is angling your direction <em>toward</em> the human zone instead of straight into the kill zone. And for that, we borrow a brain.</p>
    <p>Meet <strong>Rory Sutherland.</strong> Vice Chairman of Ogilvy, author of <em>Alchemy</em>, master of the unproductive/unpredictable quadrant. His whole thing: the modern world is obsessed with logic, but humans aren't logical, we're psychological. (Logic says make the train faster for $6 billion. Alchemy says put WiFi on it for $50 million so people enjoy the wait.) We're going to let an AI play him.</p>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="ex-tag">The Exercise · Two Steps</div>
      <p><strong>Step 1: Education.</strong> First, let the AI teach you the framework so the next answer actually lands. Copy this, paste it, read the reply.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #2 · The Rory Education Prompt</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">Act as Rory Sutherland. I want to understand the core thesis of your book Alchemy.

Specifically, explain the difference between 'Logical' thinking and 'Psycho-logical' thinking.

Give me 3 famous examples from the book where a 'bad' or 'illogical' idea beat a logical one (like Red Bull or the London Underground). Explain why they worked despite the data saying they shouldn't.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="ex-tag">Step 2 · Application</div>
      <p>Now turn that lens on your own direction. Type the role, idea, or candidate direction you want to pressure-test, then copy the prompt.</p>
      <label class="wb-label inline" for="f-direction">The role, career goal, or business idea you're testing</label>
      <input id="f-direction" class="wb-input" data-field="direction" type="text" placeholder="e.g., CAD training for small manufacturers" autocomplete="off">
    </div>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #3 · The Rory Application Prompt</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">Now, acting as Rory Sutherland, analyze my situation.

My Context: <span class="ph" data-ph="direction">[your job title, career goal, or business idea]</span>

The Logical Trap: Why is the standard 'sensible' advice for this role actually a dead end?

The Irrational Pivot: Give me a move that looks 'bad' on a resume (e.g., learning a 'useless' skill, taking a demotion to learn, focusing on unmeasurable tasks) but actually increases my value in the Human Zone.

The Supervillain Angle: Give me a polarizing take on my industry that most people are too polite to say.</div>
    </div>

    <figure class="testimonial">
      <blockquote>"Corey, this column may have saved my career. The AI Rory prompt helped put something in perspective for me about my new job and guided how I'm going to approach it going forward. I can't explain it without giving you a ton of detail, but I just wanted to say thank you!"</blockquote>
      <figcaption>— Andy Klungland</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="outcome">
      <span class="oc-tag">You walk away with</span>
      <p>Your asset list, filtered and re-angled. Now you know which directions are durable and which to walk away from <em>before</em> you sink a year into them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MODULE 3 ============ -->
  <section class="chapter module" id="m3">
    <div class="chapter-label">Module 3</div>
    <h2>Find the Crosshairs: Your Ikigai Intersection</h2>
    <p class="module-dek">Where what you love, what you're good at, what's needed, and what pays all meet.</p>

    <p>Everyone tells you to invest in your 401(k). Nobody tells you to invest in <em>yourself.</em> But the best investment you'll ever make is the one with the most control over how much comes in and how long it lasts. That's you.</p>
    <p>There's a Japanese concept that maps the sweet spot: <strong>Ikigai</strong>, your "reason for being." It sits at the overlap of four circles:</p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Passion:</strong> what excites you.</li>
      <li><strong>Proficiency:</strong> what you're good at.</li>
      <li><strong>Mission:</strong> what the world needs.</li>
      <li><strong>Vocation:</strong> what you can get paid for.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/compass/ikigai-diagram.jpg" alt="The Ikigai diagram: four overlapping circles of passion, mission, profession and vocation meeting in the middle">
      <figcaption>Ikigai: your "reason for being" sits dead center, where all four circles overlap.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>Any one circle on its own is a hobby, a job, a cause, or a paycheck. The magic is only where all four overlap. That's the direction that survives the long haul, because it passes the "work you'd do anyway" filter. Like they say: if you like what you do, you never work a day in your life. That's the test. We're picking something you'd happily still be doing in 15 years, retire or not.</p>
    <div class="pull">A direction that only pays isn't a North Star. It's just another job with extra steps.</div>
    <p>When I ran this exercise on myself, the AI spit back ideas I'd quietly already made money on without realizing they were connected: music marketing, CAD training, Google Ads consulting. It knew nothing about me except what I typed. That's how accurate this gets when you let it interview you properly.</p>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="ex-tag">The Exercise</div>
      <p>This one's a conversation, not a one-shot. Paste the prompt and the AI will interview you. Keep answering its questions honestly until it's confident enough to name where your four circles intersect <em>profitably.</em></p>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #4 · The Ikigai Coach</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">Act as an Ikigai coach by asking me detailed questions about my life. Continue asking questions until you have a complete understanding of where my passions, proficiencies, purpose, intersect in a profitable way. Your goal is to develop a deep understanding of what I love to do and how I can make money at it. Once you are extremely confident that you understand my situation well, give me advice on what business I should start while being employed full time, with a goal of eventually transitioning from my full-time employment to my business.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="protip">
      <span class="pt-tag">Pro tip</span>
      <p>Push it to really <em>interview</em> you. Answer in full sentences, not one-word replies. If it jumps to ideas too fast, push back: <em>"Ask me 5 more questions before you answer."</em> The more it digs, the sharper the directions it lands on. When it finally names them, ask: <em>"Which of these could I start this month while still employed?"</em></p>
    </div>

    <div class="outcome">
      <span class="oc-tag">You walk away with</span>
      <p>1–3 candidate directions sitting in your personal sweet spot. The question stops being "what should I do?" and becomes "which of these?"</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MODULE 4 ============ -->
  <section class="chapter module" id="m4">
    <div class="chapter-label">Module 4</div>
    <h2>Turn Your Direction Into Income: Three Ways to Get Paid</h2>
    <p class="module-dek">A direction isn't an income. Here's how it becomes one.</p>

    <p>You've got a direction. Now comes the part that pays the bills: turning it into income. For solo online income, there are three main ways to do that. Not literally every way to earn a living, just the three that fit a full-time professional testing something on the side. Pick the one that fits where you're at right now. You can add the others later:</p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Path 1: Freelance it.</strong> Sell your time. Often the fastest path to real money for mid-career professionals, because you're renting out skills you already have.</li>
      <li><strong>Path 2: Package it.</strong> Sell something you create once (a course, a template, a product). More work up front, but it can keep selling while you sleep.</li>
      <li><strong>Path 3: Build an audience.</strong> Start a newsletter. It usually pays the least at the start and has the highest potential ceiling, because it's the one audience asset you truly <em>own.</em> Not a follower count you rent from an algorithm that can cut your reach overnight.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="cheat">
      <div class="cheat-tag">Which path first?</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Want extra cash in the next 30–90 days and already have work experience? → Start with <strong>Path 1 (Freelance).</strong></li>
        <li>Like creating things and can wait a bit for the money? → <strong>Path 2 (Package).</strong></li>
        <li>Care about long-term freedom and don't mind slow results? → Run <strong>Path 3 (newsletter)</strong> in the background while you work on Path 1 or 2.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub">First, Check Real Demand</h3>
    <p>Don't guess at demand. Go look at it. And the move most people miss: you don't need a different method for each path. Run your skill (or your topic) through a quick keyword search and you'll see demand for <em>all three paths at once.</em></p>
    <p>Back when I was a draftsman, one afternoon of searching told me plenty. Roughly 3,600 people a month were searching <em>"drafting services"</em> (Path 1, folks who'd hire me). Another <strong>22,000</strong> a month searching <em>"AutoCAD courses"</em> (Path 2, folks who'd buy a course). And a steady stream looking for industry how-to (Path 3, folks who'd read a newsletter). One search, three signals.</p>

    <p>A few free ways to read it:</p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Ubersuggest</strong> (free). Type in your skill or topic and see how many people search it each month.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Trends</strong> (free). Is interest rising, flat, or fading?</li>
      <li><strong>Plain Google.</strong> If you see sponsored ads on a term, businesses are paying to show up there. That's a profit signal.</li>
      <li><strong>Marketplace reviews</strong> (Udemy, Amazon). Rough rule: only about 10% of buyers leave a review, so multiply the review count by ~10 to estimate buyers. A course with thousands of reviews is proof people pay.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Here's me walking through the whole thing on my old draftsman background, finding real opportunity with nothing but free Google tools:</p>

    <div class="video-embed">
      <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/wLfjnkykO1Q" title="Using free Google tools to find demand for your skills" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">Free-tools demand check, start to finish, using my old draftsman career as the example.</p>

    <p>Want the AI to do the legwork? Paste this into a tool with web or deep-research turned on (ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini), and it'll pull the demand picture together for you.</p>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #5 · Demand Research</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">You are a market-demand researcher with live web access. I'm a professional weighing a new direction, and I need an honest, specific read on real demand before I invest my time.

My candidate direction: <span class="ph" data-ph="direction">[your direction]</span>
My background and skills: <span class="ph" data-ph="skills">[your skills]</span>

Research and report back:
1. Search interest — is demand for this rising, flat, or fading? Give rough monthly search volume for the terms people would actually type.
2. Who's already getting paid — are freelancers hired for it (and at what rates)? Are people selling courses or products about it (and roughly how many buyers)? Are creators or newsletters covering it?
3. Where the audience gathers — which subreddits, groups, or forums, and the top 3 questions or complaints they repeat.
4. A demand verdict — Strong, Mixed, or Thin — with the evidence behind it.
5. The single fastest, cheapest way I could test this in the next two weeks.

Be specific and cite what you find. If you're unsure about something, say so instead of guessing.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="protip">
      <span class="pt-tag">Reality check</span>
      <p>This only works with web or research mode switched on. And AI can be confidently wrong. Before you bet on its answer, verify the top finding against one real source: an Ubersuggest search, a Google Trends graph, or the Upwork scan below.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub">Then, for Path 1: The Upwork Check</h3>
    <p>If freelancing is your first move, Upwork is the best hands-on check. It shows what clients <em>actually pay.</em> Scan the <strong>Jobs</strong> and <strong>Talent</strong> sections for four things:</p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li>Types of projects that match your experience</li>
      <li>Services others offer that feel similar to what you could do</li>
      <li>Typical hourly or project rates</li>
      <li>Recurring patterns in titles, skills, and niches</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Jot down what you see in the box below. It feeds your Path 1 prompt.</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/compass/upwork-jobs-search.avif" alt="An example Upwork search showing real freelance jobs and the rates clients are paying">
      <figcaption>A quick Upwork search shows you real jobs and real rates, not guesses.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="proof-note">
      <strong>One example, not a promise:</strong> when I went back to Upwork after a slow stretch, I committed to a focused 30-day run. 12 proposals sent → 8 meetings booked → 4 clients landed → $1,602.60 in revenue (and counting). Not a guarantee. Just an example of what's possible using skills I already had.
    </div>

    <figure class="fig small">
      <img src="/images/compass/upwork-job-success.avif" alt="Corey's Upwork profile showing a 100% job success score">
      <figcaption>100% job-success score. Proof you can build a reputation from a standing start.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>And set your expectations honestly. Your first few proposals will go nowhere. That's normal. Treat the first 10–20 as practice while you learn how clients talk about their problems.</p>

    <!-- ── Upwork findings input ── -->
    <div class="workbench secondary">
      <div class="wb-tag">What You Found on Upwork</div>
      <p class="wb-intro">Rough notes are fine. These feed the validation prompt below.</p>

      <label class="wb-label" for="f-jobsFound">Types of jobs that seem related to your skills</label>
      <textarea id="f-jobsFound" class="wb-input" data-field="jobsFound" rows="2" placeholder="e.g., CAD drafting, technical writing, process documentation…"></textarea>

      <label class="wb-label" for="f-servicesFound">Common services other freelancers are offering</label>
      <textarea id="f-servicesFound" class="wb-input" data-field="servicesFound" rows="2" placeholder="e.g., 2D-to-3D modeling, equipment manuals, SOP creation…"></textarea>

      <label class="wb-label" for="f-ratesFound">Typical hourly rates or fixed prices you saw</label>
      <textarea id="f-ratesFound" class="wb-input" data-field="ratesFound" rows="2" placeholder="e.g., $35–$90/hr, $300–$1,200 per project…"></textarea>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub">Path 1: Freelance It</h3>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="ex-tag">The Exercise · Path 1</div>
      <p>Copy this, fill in the brackets, and paste it into your favorite AI tool. It returns 5–10 specific offers you could realistically make, ranked by which one you could test the fastest.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #6 · Freelance Validation</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">You are an experienced career coach and freelance consultant who specializes in helping people over 50 turn their skills into paid freelance services.

I am <span class="ph" data-ph="age">[your age]</span> years old and I want to explore using Upwork to earn extra income on the side without quitting my job (yet).

Here is my background and experience:
- Past jobs and roles: <span class="ph" data-ph="roles">[list them]</span>
- Skills I use at work: <span class="ph" data-ph="skills">[list them]</span>
- Software / tools I know: <span class="ph" data-ph="tools">[list them]</span>
- Hobbies or side projects where I've developed real skill: <span class="ph" data-ph="hobbies">[list them]</span>

Here is what I found when I searched Upwork for people like me:
- Types of jobs that seem related to my skills: <span class="ph" data-ph="jobsFound">[list them]</span>
- Common services other freelancers are offering: <span class="ph" data-ph="servicesFound">[list them]</span>
- Typical hourly rates or fixed prices I saw: <span class="ph" data-ph="ratesFound">[list them]</span>

Using this information, please:
1. Suggest 5–10 specific freelance services I could realistically offer on Upwork.
2. For each service, explain briefly why it fits my background.
3. Suggest a beginner-friendly price range for each service based on what you see people charging on Upwork.
4. Rank the services by 'fastest to test' (easiest to get in front of real clients quickly).</div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub">Path 2: Package It</h3>
    <p>Here's the shift that makes Path 2 click. Say you build financial models in Excel at your day job. As a freelancer (Path 1) you might charge $50 an hour to build them for clients. <em>Package</em> that same skill instead. Turn your best model into a plug-and-play template, and you can sell it for $29 over and over while you sleep. Same expertise, built once and sold many times.</p>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="ex-tag">The Exercise · Path 2</div>
      <p>Copy this into an AI assistant like ChatGPT or Claude, fill in your details, and let it propose 5 specific "build-once, sell-twice" ideas.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-prompt">
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-label">Plug-in Prompt #7 · Build-Once, Sell-Twice Ideas</span>
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-text">I am <span class="ph" data-ph="age">[your age]</span> years old with the following skills: <span class="ph" data-ph="skills">[your skills]</span>; and this background: <span class="ph" data-ph="roles">[your roles]</span>. I want to create a 'build-once, sell-twice' digital product. Based on my background, give me 5 specific ideas for templates, guides, or mini-courses I could create. For each idea, tell me who the exact target customer is and what specific problem it solves for them.</div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub">Path 3: Build an Audience</h3>
    <p>No prompt for this one. It's less an offer you validate and more the <em>asset you build underneath</em> whichever path you pick.</p>
    <p>The third way in is the slow one: start a newsletter. It usually pays the least at the start and has the highest potential ceiling over the long term, because it's the one thing you actually <em>own.</em> Not a follower count you rent from an algorithm that can cut your reach overnight. An audience that's <em>yours</em>, one that makes your freelancing and your products compound instead of starting from a cold stop every time.</p>
    <p>And here's why it's especially powerful for someone in your shoes. As a 50‑something professional, you've already built the skills and the stories. Your newsletter turns that hard-won experience into a magnet for the right people.</p>
    <p>An audience also pays in more ways than one. Once people trust you, you can earn from sponsorships, affiliate recommendations, a course, a paid community, or your own services. Often several at once. That's what makes it the highest-ceiling path: it's not a single income stream, it's the soil the others grow in. (How to actually build and monetize it is its own project. That's where the next step picks up.)</p>
    <p>The move the pros make: you don't pick this <em>instead</em> of the first two paths. You run it <em>underneath</em> them. Start collecting emails from day one, so every client, product, and idea you ever have has somewhere warm to land. That's a build of its own, though, and it's exactly where this whole thing points once you've named your direction. <strong>More on that at the finish line.</strong></p>

    <h3 class="sub">Zoom Out: The Bigger Picture</h3>
    <p>This is the part that takes the pressure off picking "the one." You don't have to. The three paths are really <em>rungs on a ladder</em> you climb over time. Start on one, add the next when you're ready.</p>

    <div class="ladder-card">
      <div class="ladder-tag">The Value Ladder</div>
      <div class="ladder-plot">
        <div class="ladder-yaxis"><span>Value →</span></div>
        <div class="ladder-steps">
          <div class="ladder-step s1"><span class="ladder-price">Free</span></div>
          <div class="ladder-step s2"><span class="ladder-price">$</span></div>
          <div class="ladder-step s3"><span class="ladder-price">$ ×4</span></div>
          <div class="ladder-step s4"><span class="ladder-price">$ ×10</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ladder-xaxis">Price →</div>
      <div class="ladder-recurring">
        <span class="lr-label">Recurring</span>
        <span class="lr-arrows">→&nbsp;$&nbsp;&nbsp;→&nbsp;$&nbsp;&nbsp;→&nbsp;$</span>
        <span class="lr-note">memberships &amp; retainers can run underneath</span>
      </div>
      <ul class="ladder-legend">
        <li><strong>Free:</strong> your newsletter / audience</li>
        <li><strong>$:</strong> a small first offer (a template, a mini-service)</li>
        <li><strong>$ ×4:</strong> a course or group program</li>
        <li><strong>$ ×10:</strong> premium (done-for-you or coaching)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="ladder-caption">This is Perry Marshall's "espresso machine" idea from <em>80/20 Sales and Marketing</em>. In any market, plenty of folks happily buy the $1 gas-station coffee. But a slice will pay for the $50 bag of beans, the $500 home machine, even the $5,000 setup. There's <em>always</em> someone who wants the premium version. So each rung runs roughly 4–10× the last: same audience, ascending value. You don't build all of it now. You climb it over years.</p>

    <div class="outcome">
      <span class="oc-tag">You walk away with</span>
      <p>A clear first path to start down · a short list of demand-validated offers, ranked by how fast you could test each one · and a simple game plan you can run for the next 90 days instead of staying stuck in your head.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Skill-gap detour (troubleshooting safety net) ── -->
    <h3 class="sub">What If You Get Stuck?</h3>
    <p class="door-three"><strong>Don't have the skill yet?</strong> If you pick a path and realize you're missing a piece, that's not a wall. It's a short detour. Upwork regularly publishes lists of "most in-demand" and "fastest-growing" skills. Recent lists lean heavily toward AI-related work and automation, but they change every year, so always check the latest one.</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/compass/upwork-in-demand-skills.gif" alt="Upwork's list of in-demand freelance skills">
      <figcaption>Upwork publishes its in-demand skills regularly. (This snapshot is older. The list changes every year, so always pull the current one. Lately it leans AI and automation.)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>Then go learn it. Pick a highly-rated course on a site like Udemy and work through it. Many practical freelance skills can be learned well enough in a few weeks to handle beginner-level projects, as long as you practice and stay honest about what you can do. Finishing a solid course often puts you well ahead of the client who's hiring, because they usually just know they have a problem, not how to solve it.</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/compass/chatgpt-courses.jpg" alt="Thousands of ChatGPT courses on Udemy with tens of thousands of students enrolled">
      <figcaption>Over 5,000 courses on just one AI topic, with tens of thousands enrolled. Whatever the skill, someone's already teaching it.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>And if the work ever dries up or you just get bored, you run the same loop again: <em>find a skill in demand → learn enough to be useful → offer a simple, clear service.</em> That's what keeps you future-proof.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MODULE 5 ============ -->
  <section class="chapter module" id="m5">
    <div class="chapter-label">Module 5</div>
    <h2>Set the Heading: Your North Star Page</h2>
    <p class="module-dek">One direction. On paper. With a first step you take this week.</p>

    <p>This is where it all lands. Pull from everything your AI handed you across the four modules and fill in the one-pager below. Short answers. Plain words. This is the document you walk away with.</p>

    <p>Not sure what "good" looks like? Here's how it came out for two very different people. One leaned on decades of work experience, the other on a lifelong passion. Same five questions, two completely different lives:</p>

    <div class="northstar sample">
